{"id":"neet-2020-q018","pn":18,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","st":"Inflorescence and ovary position","q":"Rayflorets have","o":["Superior ovary","Hypogynous ovary","Half inferiour ovary","Inferior ovary"],"c":3,"s":"Ray florets of sunflower (Asteraceae) have an inferior ovary."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q020","pn":20,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","st":"Types of roots","q":"The roots that originate from the base of the stem are","o":["Primary roots","Prop roots","Lateral roots","Fibrous roots"],"c":3,"s":"In monocots the primary root is short-lived.\nA cluster of roots arises from the base of the stem; these are fibrous roots."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q076","pn":76,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","st":"Ovule","q":"The body of the ovule is fused within the funicle","o":["Micropyle","Nucellus","Chalaza","Hilum"],"c":3,"s":"The hilum is the junction where the ovule body is fused with the funicle."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q087","pn":87,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","st":"Ovary position","q":"The ovary is half inferior in :","o":["Mustard","Sunflower","Plum","Brinjal"],"c":2,"s":"Plum (rose family) has a perigynous flower with half inferior ovary.\nMustard and brinjal have superior ovary; sunflower has inferior ovary."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q028","pn":28,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","st":"Placentation","q":"Placentation, in which ovules develop on the inner wall of the ovary or in peripheral part, is :","o":["Parietal","Free central","Basal","Axile"],"c":0,"s":"In parietal placentation the ovules sit on the inner wall of the ovary.\nThe ovary is one-chambered at first.\nNote: The paper prints no solution; this working is added here."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q119","pn":119,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","st":"Root modifications","q":"Pneumatophores occur in","o":["Halophytes","Free-floating hydrophytes","Carnivorous plants","Submerged hydrophytes"],"c":0,"s":"Pneumatophores are upward-growing roots with pores that take in air.\nThey occur in halophytes (mangroves) growing in marshy, oxygen-poor soil."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q120","pn":120,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","st":"Root modifications","q":"Sweet potato is a modified","o":["Stem","Adventitious root","Tap root","Rhizome"],"c":1,"s":"Sweet potato (Ipomoea batatas) is a tuberous root that grows from the stem nodes, so it is an adventitious root.\nPotato, in contrast, is a stem tuber."},
{"id":"neet-2017-q064","pn":64,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","st":"Fruits","q":"Coconut fruit is","o":["Capsule","Drupe","Berry","Nut"],"c":1,"s":"Coconut is a one-seeded fruit with an outer fibrous mesocarp and a hard stony endocarp.\nThis kind of fruit is a drupe."},
{"id":"neet-2017-q066","pn":66,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","st":"Root regions","q":"Root hairs develop from the region of :","o":["Meristematic activity","Maturation","Elongation","Root cap"],"c":1,"s":"Root hairs form from epidermal cells in the region of maturation.\nThey increase the surface for absorption of water and minerals."},
{"id":"neet-2017-q070","pn":70,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","st":"Stem modifications","q":"In Bougainvillea thorns are the modifications of :","o":["Leaf","Stipules","Adventitious root","Stem"],"c":3,"s":"Thorns of Bougainvillea and Citrus arise from axillary buds.\nSo they are modified stems."},
{"id":"neet-2017-q125","pn":125,"y":"NEET 2017 · 7 May","tp":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","st":"Fruit and seed","q":"The morphological nature of edible part of coconut is :","o":["Pericarp","Perisperm","Cotayledon","Endosperm"],"c":3,"s":"The coconut water is free-nuclear endosperm and the white kernel is cellular endosperm.\nBoth are edible parts and are endosperm."}
